1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,913
Two programs both want the processor, at the same moment. Who goes first?

2
00:00:04,933 --> 00:00:06,652
On Linux, you get to decide.

3
00:00:07,122 --> 00:00:09,641
In this video: adjust process scheduling.

4
00:00:14,161 --> 00:00:16,744
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:17,214 --> 00:00:18,794
Adjust process scheduling.

6
00:00:19,264 --> 00:00:21,927
We'll start a process with a chosen priority,

7
00:00:21,947 --> 00:00:24,969
change the priority of one that is already running,

8
00:00:24,989 --> 00:00:27,116
and see the effect on the processor.

9
00:00:36,636 --> 00:00:41,544
The scheduler is the part of the kernel that decides which process runs next.

10
00:00:41,564 --> 00:00:45,896
Each process has a niceness, a number from minus twenty to nineteen.

11
00:00:45,916 --> 00:00:47,176
The default is zero.

12
00:01:01,646 --> 00:01:05,511
A high number means a nice process: it lets others go first.

13
00:01:05,531 --> 00:01:09,915
A low number means a greedy process, which gets the processor first.

14
00:01:09,935 --> 00:01:12,570
This only matters when processes compete.

15
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:23,551
Anyone can make their own processes nicer.

16
00:01:23,571 --> 00:01:26,865
Only root can lower the number below the current value.

17
00:01:32,385 --> 00:01:36,466
The command nice, with nothing after it, prints the niceness of the shell:

18
00:01:36,486 --> 00:01:37,464
zero, the default.

19
00:01:43,934 --> 00:01:48,059
nice with the option -n 10 starts a command with niceness ten.

20
00:01:48,079 --> 00:01:51,402
Here the command is nice itself, so it prints ten.

21
00:01:58,922 --> 00:02:03,047
Now we create competition. Our machine has two processor cores.

22
00:02:03,067 --> 00:02:07,718
We start two normal yes processes, the same trick as in the last video:

23
00:02:07,738 --> 00:02:09,889
a busy process in the background.

24
00:02:19,359 --> 00:02:24,864
And two more, started with nice -n 19. Nineteen is the nicest value there is.

25
00:02:34,384 --> 00:02:39,316
Now ps, with -e for every process and -o to choose the columns.

26
00:02:39,336 --> 00:02:44,583
This time we ask for NI, the niceness, between the PID and the CPU.

27
00:02:53,053 --> 00:02:58,101
Look at the CPU column. The two normal processes take almost a full core each.

28
00:02:58,121 --> 00:03:01,285
The two with niceness nineteen get a few percent.

29
00:03:01,305 --> 00:03:04,339
The nice ones wait, because the cores are busy.

30
00:03:13,859 --> 00:03:16,976
You can change a running process with renice.

31
00:03:16,996 --> 00:03:21,089
The option -n gives the new niceness, and -p gives the PID.

32
00:03:21,109 --> 00:03:23,738
We make the first yes nicer, nineteen.

33
00:03:33,208 --> 00:03:39,246
renice prints the old and the new priority. And ps -p confirms: niceness nineteen.

34
00:03:46,766 --> 00:03:48,342
Now the other direction.

35
00:03:48,362 --> 00:03:53,930
We try to give a nice process a negative niceness, minus five, so it becomes greedy.

36
00:03:53,950 --> 00:03:56,325
As a normal user: permission denied.

37
00:04:02,795 --> 00:04:08,459
With sudo it works: old priority nineteen, new priority minus five. ps shows it.

38
00:04:08,479 --> 00:04:12,439
Only root can raise a process above the normal priority.

39
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:25,395
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

40
00:04:26,915 --> 00:04:32,535
We stop the yes processes with pkill. Then we start a sleep with niceness ten.

41
00:04:32,555 --> 00:04:37,668
ps -C selects processes by command name, and it shows the niceness ten.

42
00:04:46,138 --> 00:04:49,462
We reboot. Afterwards, ps and pgrep show nothing:

43
00:04:49,482 --> 00:04:55,057
a reboot ends the process, and the niceness goes with it. Niceness is never saved.

44
00:05:04,577 --> 00:05:10,626
Your turn. Start sleep 600 in the background. Start sleep 601 with nice -n 5.

45
00:05:10,646 --> 00:05:14,725
Then use renice to give sleep 600 a niceness of ten,

46
00:05:14,745 --> 00:05:17,720
and run the checker before you reboot.

47
00:05:24,190 --> 00:05:25,940
Try before you open the solution.

48
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:33,680
Next up: manage tuning profiles.

49
00:05:34,150 --> 00:05:37,053
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
